-- title:
--   Atkin–Lehner swaps the two component coordinates of reduction
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p \neq 2$ and a positive integer $M$ with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$, and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing the kernel of the reduction map $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ (hypothesis `hHp`). A set $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is given, together with the hypothesis `hin : HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`, which asserts that for every prime $\ell$ the condition `HeckeInputsHAlong` holds over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ at level $(M,H)$ and degree $\ell$, and that for every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ there is an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot F(\Gamma_H(M))$ realising the diamond operator $\langle d\rangle$. Here $J_H(M)$ denotes `JH M H`, the group $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$.
--
--   Hecke data: a commutative ring $\mathbb{T}$ which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra acting on the Tate module $T_p(J_H(M))$ (the group of sequences $(x_n)$ in $J_H(M)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p\,x_{n+1} = x_n$) compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-action, such that the action is faithful (`hfaith`: an element of $\mathbb{T}$ killing every element of the Tate module is zero); a map `op` from the generators [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the symbols $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \notin S$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, and $\langle d\rangle$ for $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$) to $\mathbb{T}$ with `hop`: `op g` acts on the Tate module as the endomorphism induced by `genOpH M H S g`, and `hgen`: the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of `op` is all of $\mathbb{T}$. Further, an idempotent splitting $S'$ of $\mathbb{T}$ (a finite family of complete orthogonal idempotents $e_i$ together with maximal ideals $\mathfrak{m}_i$ exhausting the maximal ideals of $\mathbb{T}$, with $e_i \in \mathfrak{m}_j$ exactly when $i \neq j$), an index $i_0$, and the hypothesis `hord` that $\mathrm{op}(U_p) \notin \mathfrak{m}_{i_0}$.
--
--   Geometric data: a valuation subring $Pl$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ (that is, $p$ belongs to its non-units), with algebraically closed residue field of characteristic $p$; the hypothesis `hj` that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the $q$-expansion function field of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ over $\mathbb{Q}$; a Deligne–Rapoport-type integral model $\mathfrak{X} :$ `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` over the localised base `R p`, carrying in particular the geometric curve model $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot F(\Gamma_H(M))$ with its bijection `pointEquivPlace` between $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points and places, and the Atkin–Lehner automorphism $\mathfrak{X}.w$; level data $\Lambda$ at $Pl$, consisting of a base point $\Lambda.\sigma_A$, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ over the base with relative group law $\Lambda.L$ and dictionaries for generic and special points. The hypothesis `hrepΛ` asserts that the designation built from $\Lambda.X$, $\Lambda.f$ and the unit section of $\Lambda.L$ represents the relative Picard functor of rigidified line bundles fibrewise algebraically equivalent to zero on the level-$\Gamma_N$ curve `toBase p (ΓN p M H hpM) hj`, rigidified along the composite of $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ with $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$. Finally $O$ is a Néron object `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ` for $J_H(M)$: a smooth separated scheme $O.G$ over the base `base p` with relative group law $O.L$, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from $J_H(M)$ to sections of $O.g$ over the geometric generic point, Hecke endomorphisms $O.\mathrm{hecke}$, a special-fibre dictionary $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}$ identifying the glued Picard group $\mathrm{GluedPic}^0$ of the residue function field `Fbar p M H hpM (ResidueField Pl)` along the finite set $O.\mathrm{ssFinset}$ of pairs of places with the sections of $O.g$ over `resPt Pl ≫ Λ.σA`, a toric rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$, and the subgroups $O.\mathrm{finPts}(m)$ (generated by the $m$-torsion classes whose associated generic section extends over $Pl$) and $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(m)$.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi pinning (the group `hD`, `hDQ`, `hsep`, `ajQ`, `kQ`, `ajbar`, `εbar`, `hpoinc`, `hajQε`, `hajQ`, `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`, `hajbar`, `hajbar_over`, `hεbar`, `hεbar_aj`, `hpts_law`, `hAJ`, summarised here): $(O.G, O.g)$ with the unit section of $O.L$ represents the algebraic-equivalence-zero part of the relative Picard functor of the curve `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$, and likewise after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$, where the base-changed curve is separated; $ajQ$ is a section over the base-changed curve whose pullback of the Poincaré bundle is, at every point with values in a field, isomorphic to the line bundle of that point tensored with the ideal module of the $\infty$-section (`hajQ`), which restricts along the $\infty$-section to the zero section (`hajQε`); the Poincaré bundles over $\mathbb{Q}$ and over `R p` agree up to isomorphism (`hpoinc`); $kQ$ compares the pullbacks along the geometric generic point and along $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathbb{Q}$ (`hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂`); $\overline{aj} : \mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C \to O.G$ is the composite of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{eeta}$, $kQ$, $ajQ$ and the first projection (`hajbar`), lies over the generic point (`hajbar_over`), and $\overline{\varepsilon}$ is a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ lying above the $\infty$-section (`hεbar`) and sent by $\overline{aj}$ to the unit (`hεbar_aj`); $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law supplied by `hD` (`hpts_law`); and `hAJ` states that for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$, $s$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ with $s$ lying above the $\infty$-section there is a degree-zero divisor $D$ equal to $(\mathrm{place}\,x) - (\mathrm{place}\,s)$ whose class satisfies $(O.\mathrm{pts}[D])_1 = x \text{ followed by } \overline{aj}$.
--
--   The unramified base ring (the group `hRA`, `hRdvr`, `hRirr`, `hRfix`, `hRmax`): $R$ is a Henselian local domain with algebraically closed residue field, an $R$-algebra structure on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with faithful scalar multiplication, whose image lies in $Pl$, which is a discrete valuation ring in which $p$ is irreducible, and which is characterised inside $Pl$ by inertia: an automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q}$ lies in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$ precisely when it fixes $R$ pointwise, and every element of $Pl$ fixed by the inertia subgroup comes from $R$.
--
--   The $p$-divisible group data: $\mathcal{G}$ is a $p$-divisible group over $R$ of height $h$ (a tower of finite free cocommutative Hopf algebras $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ with surjective transitions, ranks $p^{vh}$, and the expected torsion-ideal kernels), $\Delta$ an injective additive map from its $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points to $J_H(M)$ whose image at level $v$ is exactly $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ (`hΔlev`), which is equivariant for the Galois action through $R$-automorphisms (`hΔgal`) and equivariant for each Hecke generator via a compatible family of $R$-coalgebra endomorphisms of the levels (`hΔhecke`).
--
--   The toric quotient data: a second $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $R$ of height $h_B$ with maps $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, together with $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$, $h_B = 2h'$, compatibility of $\psi$ with the transition maps (`hψt`), the statement that a level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ has trivial $\psi$-image exactly when its $\Delta$-image lies in $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ (`hψker`), surjectivity of $\psi$ on points (`hψsurj`), the implication that if the $\psi$-image of a point reduces to the identity (all values of the associated $R$-algebra map differ from the counit by elements of valuation $< 1$) then so does the point itself (`hψred`), and the period condition `hperiod`: for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $Pl$, every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ in $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ and every level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma \cdot z - z$, the $\psi$-image of $y$ reduces to the identity.
--
--   The immersion data: a ring map $\rho_h : \mathrm{R}\,p \to R$ compatible with the embeddings into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρh`), and morphisms $\iota_v : \mathrm{Spec}\,(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ lying over $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $R$ via $\rho_h$ (`hιbase`), whose induced lift into the fibre product is a closed immersion (`hιcl`), which land in the $p^v$-torsion (`hιp`), are compatible with $O.\mathrm{pts}\circ\Delta$ (`hιpts`), are homomorphisms for $O.L$ on points with values in any $R$-algebra (`hιmul`), are compatible with the transition maps (`hιt`), intertwine the Hecke coalgebra endomorphisms with $O.\mathrm{hecke}$ while matching $\Delta$ and `genOpH` (`hιhecke`), and `hιfin`: the resulting morphism $j_v$ from $\mathrm{Spec}\,(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ to the pullback over $\mathrm{Spec}\,R$ of the $p^v$-torsion of $O.G$ is both an open and a closed immersion, and every point of that pullback lying over the closed point of $R$ is in its image.
--
--   The Atkin–Lehner reading: $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ is a semilinear automorphism of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot F(\Gamma_H(M))$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (a pair of ring automorphisms of the function field and of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ compatible with the structure map), and `hwgen` states that whenever two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $y, y'$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ satisfy that $y'$ followed by the chart embedding and $\mathfrak{X}.w$ agrees with $y$ followed by the chart embedding, then $\mathrm{place}(y') = w_{\mathrm{gen}} \cdot \mathrm{place}(y)$. Moreover $\rho : \mathrm{R}\,p \to Pl$ is a ring map compatible with the embedding into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ (`hρ`) and $\Lambda.\sigma_A = \mathrm{Spec}\,\rho$ (`hσA`). Finally $\overline{W}$ is an additive endomorphism of $J_H(M)$ given by the action of $w_{\mathrm{gen}}$ (`hWbar`).
--
--   The reduction dictionary `hsp` on configured pairs: for each $i \in \{0,1\}$, each pair of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $y_1, y_2$ of $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Meta}.C$ lifted to sections $u_1, u_2$ of the integral model over $\mathrm{Spec}\,\rho$ whose images lie in the smooth locus and which agree with $y_1, y_2$ after composition with `barPt Pl`, each pair of residue-field points $u_{\kappa 1}, u_{\kappa 2}$ of the fibre compatible with $u_1, u_2$ and sections of the fibre structure map, each pair of closed points $P_1, P_2$ of the fibre curve model $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{Mfib}$ mapping under the $i$-th component to the closed points determined by $u_{\kappa 1}, u_{\kappa 2}$, each degree-zero divisor $Dv = (\mathrm{place}\,y_1) - (\mathrm{place}\,y_2)$, and each admissible gluing datum $x$ whose first divisor coordinate is $(\mathrm{place}\,P_1) - (\mathrm{place}\,P_2)$ if $i = 0$ and $0$ otherwise, whose second divisor coordinate is $(\mathrm{place}\,P_1) - (\mathrm{place}\,P_2)$ if $i = 1$ and $0$ otherwise, and whose unit coordinate is $0$: there exists a section $s$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ with $(O.\mathrm{pts}[Dv])_1 = \mathrm{barPt}\,Pl$ followed by $s$, and with $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of the restriction of $s$ along `resPt Pl` equal to the class of $x$ in $\mathrm{GluedPic}^0$.
--
--   Under all these hypotheses the conclusion is: for every $x \in O.\mathrm{finPts}(p)$ and all sections $s, s'$ of $O.g$ over $\Lambda.\sigma_A$ such that $(O.\mathrm{pts}\,x)_1$ equals $\mathrm{barPt}\,Pl$ followed by $s$ and $(O.\mathrm{pts}\,(\overline{W}x))_1$ equals $\mathrm{barPt}\,Pl$ followed by $s'$, the first coordinate of $\mathrm{toPic}^0\mathrm{Pair}$ applied to $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of the restriction of $s'$ along `resPt Pl` vanishes if and only if the second coordinate of $\mathrm{toPic}^0\mathrm{Pair}$ applied to $O.\mathrm{ptsSp}^{-1}$ of the restriction of $s$ along `resPt Pl` vanishes. Here $\mathrm{toPic}^0\mathrm{Pair}$ sends the class of an admissible gluing datum to the pair of divisor classes of its two divisor coordinates, so the two coordinates record the contributions of the two components of the special fibre.
--
--   At a prime $p$ exactly dividing the level $M$, the Atkin–Lehner involution $w_p$ interchanges the two components of the special fibre of the Deligne–Rapoport model of $X_H(M)$ at $p$; the statement records the resulting exchange of the two component coordinates of the reduction of a $p$-torsion point, in the form: the first coordinate of the reduction of $\overline{W}x$ vanishes exactly when the second coordinate of the reduction of $x$ vanishes. It feeds the analysis of points lying in a corner of the special fibre that is used in the study of the $p$-torsion of $J_H(M)$ at such a place.
